␀8Shift the hue of all colors.¶·␀␀␀␀FPushes the intensity of all colors.¶·␀␀␀␀ZAffects the luminance across the whole image.¶·␀␀␀␀rAffects the red channel intensity across the whole image.¶·␀␀␀␀vAffects the green channel intensity across the whole image.¶·␀␀␀␀tAffects the blue channel intensity across the whole image.¶·␀␀␀␀ÄÜShifts the input hue (x-axis) according to the output hue (y-axis).¶·␀␀␀␀ÄÄAdjusts saturation (y-axis) according to the input hue (x-axis).¶·␀␀␀␀ÄéAdjusts saturation (y-axis) according to the input saturation (x-axis).¶·␀␀␀␀ÄåAdjusts saturation (y-axis) according to the input luminance (x-axis).¶·␀␀␀␀fA 2D Lookup Texture (LUT) to use for color grading.¶·␀␀␀␀ÄñHow much of the lookup texture will contribute to the color grading effect.¶·␀␀␀␀ÄËUse "Gaussian" for a faster but non physical depth of field; "Bokeh" for a more realistic but slower depth of field.¶·␀␀␀␀\The distance at which the blurring will start.¶·␀␀␀␀ÄÇThe distance at which the blurring will reach its maximum radius.¶·␀␀␀␀Ä¥The maximum radius of the gaussian blur. Values above 1 may show under-sampling artifacts.¶·␀␀␀␀Ä≤Use higher quality sampling to reduce flickering and improve the overall blur smoothness.¶·␀␀␀␀FThe distance to the point of focus.¶·␀␀␀␀ÄÊThe ratio of aperture (known as f-stop or f-number). The smaller the value is, the shallower the depth of field is.¶·␀␀␀␀Ä“The distance between the lens and the film. The larger the value is, the shallower the depth of field is.¶·␀␀␀␀<The number of aperture blades.¶·␀␀␀␀Å,The curvature of aperture blades. The smaller the value is, the more visible aperture blades are. A value of 1 will make the bokeh perfectly circular.¶·␀␀␀␀VThe rotation of aperture blades in degrees.¶·␀␀␀␀ÄƒThe type of grain to use. You can select a preset or provide your own texture by selecting Custom.¶·␀␀␀␀xUse the slider to set the strength of the Film Grain effect.¶·␀␀␀␀Ä⁄Controls the noisiness response curve based on scene luminance. Higher values mean less noise in light areas.¶·␀␀␀␀Ä∏A tileable texture to use for the grain. The neutral value is 0.5 where no grain is applied.¶·␀␀␀␀0Total distortion amount.¶·␀␀␀␀ÄûIntensity multiplier on X axis. Set it to 0 to disable distortion on this axis.¶·␀␀␀␀ÄûIntensity multiplier on Y axis. Set it to 0 to disable distortion on this axis.¶·␀␀␀␀rDistortion center point. 0.5,0.5 is center of the screen.¶·␀␀␀␀ÄÙControls global screen scaling for the distortion effect. Use this to hide the screen borders when using high "Intensity."¶·␀␀␀␀ÄÍThe motion blur technique to use. If you don't need object motion blur, CameraOnly will result in better performance.¶·␀␀␀␀ÄÿThe quality of the effect. Lower presets will result in better performance at the expense of visual quality.¶·␀␀␀␀ÄòThe strength of the motion blur filter. Acts as a multiplier for velocities.¶·␀␀␀␀ÅRSets the maximum length, as a fraction of the screen's full resolution, that the velocity resulting from Camera rotation can have. Lower values will improve performance.¶·␀␀␀␀6Panini projection distance.¶·␀␀␀␀<Panini projection crop to fit.¢Å␀␀␀␀␀©·␀␀␀␀Flares¢Å␀␀␀␀␀¢Å␀␀␀␀␀©·␀␀␀␀Streaks·„␀␀¢Å␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀©·␀␀␀␀(Chromatic Abberation©·␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀Shadow Limits␀␀vStart point of the transition between shadows and midtones.¶·␀␀␀␀rEnd point of the transition between shadows and midtones.©·␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀ Highlight Limits␀␀|Start point of the transition between midtones and highlights.¶·␀␀␀␀xEnd point of the transition between midtones and highlights.¶·␀␀␀␀:The color to use for shadows.¶·␀␀␀␀@The color to use for highlights.¶·␀␀␀␀rBalance between the colors in the highlights and shadows.¶·␀␀␀␀ÄàSelect a tonemapping algorithm to use for the color grading process.¢Å␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ÄÃSpecifies the range reduction mode used when HDR output is enabled and Neutral tonemapping is enabled.¶·␀␀␀␀JUse the ACES preset for HDR displays.¶·␀␀␀␀ÅSpecify how much hue to preserve. Values closer to 0 are likely to preserve hue. As values get closer to 1, Unity doesn't correct hue shifts.¶·␀␀␀␀Å\Enable to use values detected from the output device as paper white. When enabled, output images might differ between SDR and HDR. For best accuracy, set this value manually.¶·␀␀␀␀ÅzThe reference brightness of a paper white surface. This property determines the maximum brightness of UI. The brightness of the scene is scaled relative to this value. The value is in nits.¶·␀␀␀␀Å4Enable to use the minimum and maximum brightness values detected from the output device. For best accuracy, considering calibrating these values manually.¶·␀␀␀␀Ä The minimum brightness of the screen (in nits). This value is assumed to be 0.005f with ACES Tonemap.¶·␀␀␀␀ÄÿThe maximum brightness of the screen (in nits). This value is defined by the preset when using ACES Tonemap.¶·␀␀␀␀Vignette color.¶·␀␀␀␀xSets the vignette center point (screen center is [0.5,0.5]).¶·␀␀␀␀tUse the slider to set the strength of the Vignette effect.¶·␀␀␀␀FSmoothness of the vignette borders.¶·␀␀␀␀Ä¶Should the vignette be perfectly round or be dependent on the current aspect ratio?¶·␀␀␀␀jSets the white balance to a custom color temperature.¶·␀␀␀␀ÄÇSets the white balance to compensate for a green or magenta tint.„ ␀␀␀␀␀resourceframeIndex≈ ␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀Ê‚≈ ␀␀␀␀ˇ¿␀Ê‚·„␀␀"‚␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀·„␀␀´Á␀␀␀␀␀␀␀2Shaders/2D/Light2D.shaderU¿‹␀·„␀␀´Á␀␀␀␀␀␀␀HShaders/2D/Shadow2D-Projected.shaderU¿‹␀·„␀␀´Á␀␀␀␀␀␀␀PShaders/2D/Shadow2D-Shadow-Sprite.shaderU¿‹␀·„␀␀´Á␀␀␀␀␀␀␀TShaders/2D/Shadow2D-Unshadow-Sprite.shaderU¿‹␀·„␀␀´Á␀␀␀␀␀␀␀TShaders/2D/Shadow2D-Shadow-Geometry.shaderU¿‹␀·„␀␀´Á␀␀␀␀␀␀␀XShaders/2D/Shadow2D-Unshadow-Geometry.shaderU¿‹␀·„␀␀´Á␀␀␀␀␀␀␀<Shaders/Utils/CopyDepth.shaderU¿‹␀·„␀␀´Á␀␀␀␀␀␀␀JShaders/Debug/DebugReplacement.shaderU¿‹␀·„␀␀´Á␀␀␀␀␀␀␀BShaders/Debug/HDRDebugVie