Radial: Wave originates from the position defined below!‚␀␀␀␀␀}␀␀␀␀␀∞ ␀␀␀␀␀∞ ␀␀␀␀␀!‚␀␀␀␀␀∞ ␀␀␀␀␀!‚␀␀J„␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀∞ ␀␀␀␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀ÅHAssign an optional transform to follow on the XZ axis.

This may be used if you want to use this component to read the water height at another transform's position.™·␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀A␀␀Ä§Controls how strongly the transform should rotate to align with the wave curvature¶·␀␀™·␀␀␀␀Ä Add a Y-axis rotation to the transform. Note that this technically results in an incorrect alignment.␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¥C¶·␀␀´·␀␀␀␀ÄƒSmoothly blend towards the newly calculated position and rotation. May be used to combat jittering␀␀␀␀␀␀ß·␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ÄòEnable to executing the camera following behaviour when outside of Play mode¶·␀␀␀␀ÄÏAssign a specific transform to follow on the XZ axis.

If left empty, the camera currently rendering will be targeted.ß·␀␀␀␀␀·„␀␀␀␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀Ä∂The Emission module on this particle system should have its Rate Over Distance value set 0.¶·␀␀␀␀Ä‘If this particle system is parented under a RigidBody, then assign it here for correct positional trackingß·␀␀␀␀␀´·␀␀␀␀
◊£<´·␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀•·␀␀␀␀"System Time (UTC)¶·␀␀␀␀@Material used on the tile meshes¶·␀␀␀␀ÄòWhen not in play-mode, the water will follow the scene-view camera position.¶·␀␀␀␀Ä‹If enabled, the object with the "MainCamera" tag will be assigned as the follow target when entering play mode¶·␀␀␀␀Ä The grid will follow this Transform's position on the XZ axis. Ideally set to the camera's transform.¶·␀␀␀␀`Scale of the entire grid in the length and width™·␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀öô>␀␀ A␀␀fDistance between vertices, rather higher than lower´·␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀"‚␀␀␀␀␀·„␀␀"‚␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀·„␀␀"‚␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀∞ ␀␀␀␀␀∞ ␀␀␀␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀Ö:Two completely different methods can be used to reproduce the wave height.

[GPU] Requires the "Height Pre-pass" feature to be enabled on the render feature. This queues up height samples and processes them in a compute shader, the result will be read back from the GPU asynchronously. 

Height values will represent the water surface height as it literally appears in the world, including any and all displacement effects. Slowest method, but ultimately more flexible.

[CPU] Given a water level, and water material, the same wave pattern can be 1:1 replicated through script. Does not include displacement effects and supports flat water geometry only. Fastest method¶·␀␀␀␀ÄåThis reference is required to grab the wave distance and height values¶·␀␀␀␀Å
Try to find the Water Object below or above the Transform's position. This is slower than assigning a specific Water Object directly!¶·␀␀␀␀ÄÃConfigure what should be used to set the base water level. Relative wave height is added to this value¶·␀␀␀␀ÅêThis transform's Y-position is used as the base water level, this value is important and required for correct rendering. As such, underwater rendering does not work with rivers or other non-flat water•·␀␀␀␀6GPU (Async height readback)•·␀␀␀␀<CPU (Wave pattern replication)•·␀␀␀␀.Water Object Y-position™·␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä?␀␀␀A¶·␀␀␀␀ÅF[When in Play mode] Skips processing the height prepass for the scene-view camera. This helps keep the rendering centered around the main camera when in Play mode.¶·␀␀␀␀ÅIf enabled, the reflection plane will be based on this transform's up vector (green arrow).

Otherwise the world's upwards direction is assumed¶·␀␀␀␀Ä¿Set the layers that should be rendered into the reflection. The "Water" layer is always excluded¶·␀␀␀␀Å4The renderer used by the reflection camera. It's recommend to create a separate renderer, so any custom render features aren't executed for the reflection´·␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀Ç8When disabled, the skybox reflection comes from a Reflection Probe. This has the benefit of being omni-directional rather than flat/planar. Enabled this to render the skybox into the planar reflection anyway.

Note that enabling this will override Screen Space Reflections completely!¶·␀␀␀␀Å»Render Unity's default scene fog in the reflection. Note that this doesn't strictly work correctly on large triangles, as it is incompatible with oblique camera projections.

This does not include to post-processing fog effects!¶·␀␀␀␀ÄæAlso render for the scene-view camera. This may be prone to some issues, such a console errors.™·␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä>␀␀Ä?␀␀ÅHA multiplier for the rendering resolution, based on the current screen resolution. The render scale, as configured in the pipeline settings is multiplied over this.™·␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä@␀␀Ä‚Do not render LOD objects lower than this value. Example: With a value of 1, LOD0 for LOD Groups will not be used·„␀␀␀␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀ÅdIf enabled, the center of the rendering bounds (that wraps around the water objects) moves with the Transform position

You must however ensure you are only moving on the XZ axis"‚␀␀␀␀␀∞ ␀␀␀␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀Å¨Render all transparent materials NOT on the "Water" layer into a screen-space texture, and use that for refraction rendering in the water.

If enabled, transparent materials can be submerged and refracted correctlyÊ␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀&directionalCaustics␀␀Å˙Pass on the main directional light's projection onto the water shader. Allows caustics to project along its direction (rather than top-down).

This shading operation is relative expensive, as it involves analyzing the depth texture at 4 different points·„␀␀␀␀␀·„␀␀␀␀␀Ê␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀enable␀␀Ä‰Allow SSR to be rendered in water materials that have it enabled.

Disable as a global performance scaling measureÊ␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀reflectSkybox␀␀ÇOnly enable when Reflection Probes cannot be used in a realtime lighting setup. If enabled, SSR will also reflects the skybox color and geometry in front of the water.

Ideally disabled, so that Reflection Probes can be relied on for a 1:1 accurate skybox reflection.¶·␀␀␀␀Ç‹Specifies the render method to use for the underwater rendering.

[Render Pass]: Renders the underwater rendering directly after all other Transparent materials. This also supports split-screen and multiple cameras.

[Scene Renderer]: Renders as a traditional Mesh Renderer in the scene, allowing control over the render queue. Fastest to render, recommended for VR.Ê␀␀™·␀␀␀␀size␀␀␀␀ A␀␀zD¶·␀␀™·␀␀␀␀2Distance between vertices␀␀öô>␀␀ A¶·␀␀™·␀␀␀␀Ä¨Shifts the vertices in a random direction. Definitely use this when using flat shading␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä?´·␀␀¶·␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ÇÜThe surface is normally flat, yet vertex displacement on the GPU such as waves can give the surface artificial height.

This can cause a Mesh Renderer to be prematurely culled, despite still actually being visible.

This value adds an artificial amount of height to the generate mesh's bounds, to avoid this from happening.´·␀␀•·␀␀␀␀
